\begin{theorem}
	\label{thm:128puntofijo}
	Rules $128$, $132$, $136$, $140$ always reach fixed points.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Let $x$ be an arbitrary initial configuration. Since we know that $x=0^n$ and $x=1^n$ are fixed points, let us assume that $x$ can be written as
$$x=0^{a_1}1^{b_1}\dots0^{a_k}1^{b_k}$$
such that $a_1+b_1+\dots+a_k+b_k=n$.\\
Since $f_{r}(001)=f_{r}(100)=0$, for $r\in\{128, 132, 136, 140\}$, groups of ones separated by two zeros cannot increase in size. And for these rules, groups of ones never increase in size, because $f_{128}(101)=f_{132}(101)=f_{136}(101)=f_{140}(101)=1$.\\
