On the other hand, since $f_{r}(110)=0$ for $r\in\{128, 132, 136, 140\}$, groups of $1$'s always decrease in size at the right side by at least one cell per iteration. Additionally, since $f_{128}(011)=f_{132}(011)=0$, the size of the groups will decrease faster for these rules.\\
In every iteration, groups of ones will continue to decrease in size until only isolated $1$'s remain.\\
In the case of rules $128$ and $136$ those ones will disappear finally reaching $0^n$ which is a fixed point, because $f_{128}(010)=f_{136}(010)$.\\
For rules $132$ and $140$ the isolated $1$'s will be preserved, since $f_{132}(010)=f_{140}(010)==1$; with the fixed points being written as $x=0^{a_1}10^{a_2}\dots0^{a_{k-1}}0^{a_k}1$.\\
\end{proof}

