\underline{$t=k+1$} $\ell_k$ becomes $0$, since $f_{110}=0$, with which the only surviving $1$ is on cell $s_2$, and we already know from the previous analysis what happens next.

It takes $j$ steps to go from one $1$ surrounded by zeros on the position $s_2$ to one $0$ surrounded by ones, on that same position, and it takes $j$ steps to return to the configuration we had on $t=1$ on the first analysis, and because $j+k<n$, we have found cycles $\mathcal{O}(n)$.

Using the same reasoning, we can prove that a second case with $\ell_1s_1s_2r_1$ follows the same behavior and it produces cycles of length $(j+1)+(k+1)\leq n$. Similarly, cases 3 ($r_js_1s_2r_1$) and 4 ($\ell_1s_1s_2\ell_k$) lead to cycles of length $(j+1)+k<n$ and $j+(k+1)<n$, resp.
\end{proof}
\begin{remark}
Note that cycles $\mathcal{O}(n)$ can also be found with block sequential configurations with (at least) two groups of size two or more consecutive cells that update on the same substep.
\end{remark}
