Now, we claim that 
\begin{align}\label{kuohao5.4}
|\mathbb{F}_{\eta,\nu}^\mathscr{H}(f_1,f_2,h)| \lesssim_\delta(\mathcal{B}+\nu)\mu(P_0)^{\eta+1}\prod_j \langle |f_j| \rangle_{P_0}
\end{align}

Once \eqref{kuohao5.3} and \eqref{kuohao5.4} are both valid, the desired will follow from the iterating the third term of \eqref{kuohao5.3}.


{\emph{{\textbf{\textsf Proof of \eqref{kuohao5.4}:}}}

%From (\ref{kuohao5.3}) and (\ref{kuohao5.4}) it is then seen that the collection $\mathcal{S}$ can be obtained by iterating this process, in the second step beginning with $\mathbb{S}_{\mathcal{D}(P)}^\nu\left(f_1 \chi_P, f_2 \chi_P, h \chi_P\right)$ for some $P \in \Omega$. Hence, to conclude the proof, it remains to show (\ref{kuohao5.4}). 




We establish the estimate via a Calder\'{o}n--Zygmund decomposition of the functions $f_1,f_2$, and $f_3:=h$ relative to the set \(\Omega\). For \( j = 1, 2, 3 \), we write  
\[
f_j = g_j + b_j \quad \text{where} \quad b_j := \sum_{P \in \Omega} b_{j,P} \quad \text{and} \quad b_{j,P} := \left( f_j - \langle f_j \rangle_P \right) \mathbf{1}_P.
