\subsection{Muckenhoupt weights}~~

Let \((X, \mu, \mathcal{B})\) be a ball-basis  measure space. A {weight} on \((X,\mu)\) is any measurable \(\omega\) with \(0<\omega(x)<\infty\) for \(\mu\)-a.e.\ \(x\in\bigcup_{B\in\B}B\). We define the {\tt Muckenhoupt class} $A_{p, \B}$
\begin{equation*}
[\omega]_{A_{p,\B}}:=\sup_{B \in \B} \bigg(\fint_{B} \omega\, d\mu \bigg) 
\bigg(\fint_{B}\omega^{1-p'}\, d\mu \bigg)^{p-1}<\infty,
\end{equation*} 
where \(1<p<\infty\) and \(1/p+1/p'=1\).
As for the case $p=1$, we say that $\omega\in A_{1,\B}$ if  
\begin{equation*}
[\omega]_{A_{1,\B}} := \|(M_{\B}\omega)\,\omega^{-1}\,\mathbf{1}_{X_\B}\|_{L^{\infty}(X, \mu)} <\infty.
\end{equation*}
Finally we define
\[
A_{\infty,\B}
=\bigcup_{p\ge1}A_{p,\B}, 
\quad
[\omega]_{A_{\infty,\B}}
=\inf_{\omega \in A_{p,\B}}[\omega]_{A_{p,\B}}.
\]

The following conclusions all follow from \cite{GR, ST}.
