\subsection{Max Pseudo-Likelihood}

An alternative method to estimate the values of the couplings $J_{ij}$ is the \textit{pseudolikelihood method}. As the name suggests, this technique consists in an approximation of the maximum Likelihood estimation 
\begin{equation}
    \{J_{ij}\}^{ML} = argmax_{\{J_{ij}\}}\left[P(\{\mathbf{S}\}|\{J_{ij}\}) \right]
    \label{eq:maxlikelihood}
\end{equation}

using a minimization over a loss function 
\begin{equation}
    L^{PL}(\{J_{ij}\} = \sum_{i}\frac{1}{M}\sum_{\mu = 1}^{M}\ln \frac{1}{2}\left[1 + s_{i}^{\mu} th\left(\sum_{j \neq i}J_{ij}s_{j}^{\mu}\right)\right]
\end{equation}

where the $M$ is the number of samples, the index $i$ runs over the rows of the coupling matrix and $\mu$ runs over the sample indexes. The $J^{PL}$ obtained using this method are equivalent to the $J^{ML}$ in the limit $M \to \infty$ and provide a good approximation in the $M$ finite case whenever the exact estimation using Equation \eqref{eq:maxlikelihood} is not feasible. In order to practically obtain the $\{J_{ij}\}$ matrix we will perform standard gradient descent on the couplings. \\


%%%%%%
In the case of the vectorial Potts model the Pseudo-Likelihood generalizes as 
\begin{equation}
    \mathcal{L} = -\frac{1}{M} \sum_i \sum_\mu \log \sum_{c= 0}^q \exp \left \{\beta \sum_{j \neq i}J_{ij}\vec{s}_j \cdot (\vec{s}_c - \vec{s}_i)\right \}
\end{equation}
\\
Finally, in the case of the Blume-Capel model, the pseudo-loglikelihood has the form:
\begin{equation}
    \mathcal{L} = \frac{1}{M}\sum_{i=1}^{N}\sum_{\mu=1}^{M} \left[s_i\sum_{j\neq i}^{1,N} J_{ij}s_j - \mu_i s_i^2 - \log\left( 1+2e^{-\mu_i}\cosh \sum_{j\neq i}^{1,N} J_{ij}s_j \right)\right]
\end{equation}
%%%%
One can add additional penalty terms to the pseudo-loglikelihood as regularizers. For instance, if one assumes that the couplings matrix is sparse, i.e. most of the original couplings are zero, one can add a penalty term in the form of the lasso or $\ell_1$ regularizer \cite{hastie2015statistical}:

\begin{equation}
    \mathcal{L}_\text{lasso} = \sum_{ij}|J_{ij}|,
\end{equation}
which automatically tends to lower the norm of the inferred couplings.


