Covalent-type 3D DSMs, a true analogue of graphene in 3D, were theoretically proposed in 2024~\cite{Er_In_ternary, Ga_series_synthesis,In_series_synthesis, Sato_2025} but remain virtually unexplored experimentally. They form a wide gap between valence and conduction bands due to covalent bonding, with Dirac points protected by crystal symmetry. Covalent-type 3D DSMs fall into a rather unexplored material space, as they combine atoms with similar electronegativity and weak SOC. Moreover, covalent bonding, which has a larger energy scale than SOC, pushes irrelevant bands away from the Fermi energy $E_\mathrm{F}$ due to band hybridization and realizes an extended linear energy dispersion over a wide range of energy (Fig.~\ref{Fig1} (top)). 
%, \textit{E}$_{\rm{D}}$, around 300$\,$meV. Covalent-type 3D DSMs are the true analogue of graphene, since graphene realizes its DSM state via covalent bonding, not by the SOC. 

To realize covalent-type DSMs in 3D, two main conditions are required: Atoms with similar electronegativity for strong covalent bonding and appropriate crystal symmetry to protect Dirac points from gap opening. \RX{} (\textit{R} = Y or rare earth elements, \textit{X} = Al, Ga, In) has been proposed as a candidate for covalent-type 3D DSMs~\cite{Sato_2025} with similar electronegativity between \textit{R}, Co, and \textit{X} atoms. 
While this \RX{} family had been reported as a topological material candidate in terms of symmetry~\cite{topological_catalogue_2019}, it has not attracted much attention for its covalent bonding character nor for the simplicity of its band structure.
As shown in Figs.~\ref{Fig2}(a) and (b), \RX{} crystallizes in a hexagonal structure with space group $P6_3mc$; the $6_3$ glide crystallographic symmetry along the \textit{c}-axis protects the Dirac crossing point along the $\Gamma \mathchar`-A$ line, i.e., along the \textit{k}$_z$ axis~\cite{Sato_2025}. Thus, the \RX{} satisfy the requirements for covalent-type 3D DSMs and may ultimately overcome some of the limitations of spin-orbit type DSMs.
%\textit{Introduction} -- Various topological phenomena have been found through a thorough tuning of physical parameters, especially in two-dimensional systems with a large tunability, such as electrical gating, proximity effect, or Moir\'{e}'s twist angle~\cite{QAHE, twisted_graphene, transition_metal_dichalcogenide}. In the past decade, there has been a growing interest in realizing the 3D-analogue of graphene in Weyl and Dirac semimetals. In three-dimensional (3D) systems, theoretical topological phase diagrams~\cite{Murakami_diagram, Burkov_diagram} also motivated experimental explorations for versatile topological phases over a wide range of parameters by chemical substitution~\cite{Chenlong_PnSnTe, Ilya_Weyl}, which enables us to access a wide range of parameters as compared to physical pressure or strain. However, these explorations in 3D systems have been mainly limited to low-symmetry Weyl semimetals with Weyl points of two-fold degeneracy. 



%3D Dirac semimetals (DSMs) hosting Dirac points of four-fold degeneracy have been regarded as the 3D counterpart off graphene. 3D DSMs are neighboring to various topological phases~\cite{Na3Bi_discovery}, thus various topological phases can be realized, starting from 3D DSMs. A key challenge in this field is to establish 3D DSMs with a clean Dirac state over a wide range of energy and with a large tunability in the crystal structure. The high symmetry required to protect the four-fold Dirac point has limited the choice of materials, and thus the parameter range of exploration~\cite{Na3Bi_band_calculation, Cd3As2_discovery, Cd3As2_Zndope, ZrTe5_discovery}. 

%Topological Dirac and Weyl compounds, especially when combined with electron correlations, have attracted significant interest over the past decade. Various theoretical studies have explored this topic, including the emergence of topological phases induced by strong spin-orbit coupling~\cite{SOC_correlation}, and the interplay between magnetism and Dirac/Weyl fermions~\cite{Weyl_magnetism, EuCd2As2_Dirac_theory}. These theoretical advances have been followed by numerous experimental investigations~\cite{NdAlSi, CaIrO3_Yamada_2022}.
%However, the role of electron correlations in three-dimensional Dirac semimetals (DSMs) remains largely unexplored~\cite{Na3Bi_interaction, EuCd2As2_anomolous_hall, EuCd2As2_mag_semiconductor}. One of the key challenges in previously reported three-dimensional DSMs lies in the limited tunability of these materials~\cite{Na3Bi_band_calculation, Cd3As2_discovery, Cd3As2_Zndope, ZrTe5_discovery}, as well as the narrow range of their linear energy dispersion. 

%While graphene serves as a canonical example of a two-dimensional covalent-type DSM, no material realization has been reported in 3D. \RX{} (\textit{R} = Y or rare earth elements, \textit{X} = Al, Ga, In) has been proposed as a candidate for covalent-type 3D DSMs~\cite{Sato_2025}. Similar electronegativity of \textit{R}, Co, and \textit{X} atoms realizes the strong covalent bonding in these compounds. \RX{} crystallizes in a hexagonal structure with space group $P6_3mc$ (see Fig.~\ref{Fig2}(a) and (b)). The $6_3$ glide crystallographic symmetry along the \textit{c}-axis protects the Dirac crossing point along the $\Gamma \mathchar`-A$ line on the $k_z$ axis~\cite{Sato_2025}.

%Covalent-type DSMs, a new class of 3D DSMs, were theoretically proposed in 2024 to overcome these limitations~\cite{Sato_2025}. Unlike conventional spin-orbit type DSMs, such as \NaBi{} and Cd$_3$As$_2$, which require strong spin-orbit coupling (SOC), a clean Dirac state of covalent-type DSMs is realized not by SOC, but through covalent bonding. Covalent bonding, which has a larger energy scale than SOC, pushes irrelevant bands away from the Fermi energy, realizing an extended linear energy dispersion over a wide range of energy, $E_{\rm{D}}$ (see Fig.~\ref{Fig1}, $top$). %The energy separation between the valence and conduction bands, $E_{\rm{D}}$, becomes larger, leading to an extended linear energy dispersion region.

%While graphene serves as a canonical example of a two-dimensional covalent-type DSM, no material realization has been reported in 3D. To realize 3D covalent-type DSMs, two main conditions are required: Atoms with similar electronegativity for strong covalent bonding and appropriate crystal symmetry to protect Dirac points from gap opening. \RX{} (\textit{R} = Y or rare earth elements, \textit{X} = Al, Ga, In) has been proposed as a candidate for covalent-type 3D DSMs~\cite{Sato_2025} for the similar electronegativity between \textit{R}, Co, and \textit{X} atoms. As shown in Fig.~\ref{Fig2}(a) and (b), \RX{} crystallizes in a hexagonal structure with space group $P6_3mc$, and the $6_3$ glide crystallographic symmetry along the \textit{c}-axis protects the Dirac crossing point along the $\Gamma \mathchar`-A$ line on the $k_z$ axis~\cite{Sato_2025}. Thus, \RX{} satisfy the requirements for 3D covalent-type DSMs.

As shown in Fig.~\ref{Fig1} (middle), \YIn{}, a typical example of the \RX{} family, exhibits a larger $E_{\rm{D}}$ than the representative spin-orbit type DSM \NaBi{}~\cite{Na3Bi_discovery, Na3Bi_band_calculation}. In addition to the difference in energy scale, an electron-hole symmetric gap-opening due to covalent bonding enhances $E_{\rm{D}}$ in \YIn{}, just like in graphene. This contrasts with the asymmetric dispersion in \NaBi{} due to the heavy mass of Bi 6$p$ orbitals.
Importantly -- and in stark contrast to the previously reported spin-orbit type DSMs~\cite{Cd3As2_dope, Cd3As2_dope_Sbdope} -- \RX{} are compatible with a variety of chemical substitutions on \textit{R} and \textit{X} sites as shown in  Fig.~\ref{Fig1} (bottom), offering flexibility in their band structure and magnetic properties. However, the magnetotransport properties of these materials remain unexplored so far. 

In this Letter, we use electronic transport measurements on \RX{} to report the material realization of a new category of 3D DSMs, covalent-type DSMs in the regime of small SOC. We demonstrate $E_\mathrm{F}$ tuning to within only $10 \,$meV from the Dirac point via bandwidth control by chemical substitution.
%in covalent-type 3D DSMs \RX{}. 
The carrier mobility increases in inverse proportion to $E_\mathrm{F}$, reaching high values of $3,000\,\mathrm{cm^3/Vs}$ even in polycrystalline samples and evidencing electronic transport governed by Dirac electrons. %We establish a new covalent-type DSMs \RX{} provides an ideal platform to verify theoretical toy models through systematic tuning of physical parameters of 3D DSMs.. 
Specifically, when we replace the \textit{R}-site with light rare earth elements, the ionic radius $r_{R^{3+}}$ increases by about $10 \, \%$, as shown in Fig.~\ref{Fig2}(c). The concomitant reduction of the hexagonal lattice constants promotes electron hopping, inducing a larger overlap of conduction and valence bands (see Figs.~\ref{Fig2}(d-f)). We utilize this bandwidth control to tune the $E_\mathrm{F}$ to the Dirac point as depicted in the insets to Figs.~\ref{Fig2}(d-f). %family has the substitutability in atoms with the robust Dirac crossing points protected by the crystal symmetry. Substitution of \textit{R} and \textit{X} sites induce the variety of the electronic band structure and the tunability of the Fermi energy $E_{\rm{F}}$ into the Dirac points, and also we can introduce magnetism. 
%Therefore, a covalent-type DSMs \RX{} provides an ideal platform to verify theoretical toy models through systematic tuning of physical parameters of 3D DSMs.. 

%Material introduction. explain Fig2



\begin{comment}
The electronic band structures with and without spin–orbit coupling (SOC) of Lu/Ho/Tb$_8$CoGa$_3$ are shown in Figs. 2(d–f)~\cite{Sato_2025}. In the case of Lu$_8$CoGa$_3$, only the crossing bands appear at the Fermi level, whereas for the Ho and Tb compounds, additional trivial bands are present.
This trend can be interpreted in terms of bandwidth expansion induced by the increasing ionic radius of the rare-earth elements. Within the framework of the Hubbard model, the Hamiltonian is given by
\begin{equation}
H = -t \sum_{\langle i, j \rangle, \sigma} \left( c^{\dagger}_{i\sigma} c_{j\sigma} + \mathrm{h.c.} \right) + U \sum_i n_{i\uparrow} n_{i\downarrow},
\end{equation}
where \( t \) denotes the hopping integral and \( U \) the on-site Coulomb repulsion. In the tight-binding approximation, the bandwidth \( W \) is proportional to \( t \), which in turn depends on the orbital overlap between neighboring sites. Therefore, as the ionic radius increases, the orbital overlap is enhanced, leading to an increased \( t \) and consequently a broader bandwidth. In the framework of the Hubbard model, the degree of metallicity is governed by the ratio $U/t$, thus an enlarged ionic radius effectively reduces the relative strength of electron correlation, leading to more itinerant electronic behavior and enhanced metallic character of the system.
\end{comment}



\textit{Experiment} -- \RX{} crystals were obtained by arc-melting and annealing methods~\cite{Ga_series_synthesis,In_series_synthesis}. The crystal structures of the synthesized samples were confirmed by Rietveld refinement of powder X-ray diffraction (XRD) data~\cite{SI_preprint}. The lattice volume evolves in good agreement with previous work (see Fig.~\ref{Fig2}(c))~\cite{Ga_series_synthesis,In_series_synthesis}. Resistivity measurements were carried out using a four-probe method (see inset to Fig.~\ref{Fig3}(a)).



%Explain Fig3 
\textit{Results} -- The resistivity of \RGa{} exhibits an overall metallic temperature dependence with a residual resistivity ratio of $2.5\sim3.2$ (see Fig.~\ref{Fig3}(a)). %A broad peak above $100\,$K indicates the semimetallic nature of \RGa{} likely originating from thermal activation of carriers close to the Fermi energy. 
The absolute value of the resistivity systematically decreases with rare-earth ionic radius, in accord with the expected bandwidth expansion.  

%\begin{figure*}[htb!]
To extract transport parameters such as the carrier density and carrier mobility, we measure the longitudinal ($\rho_{xx}$) and Hall ($\rho_{yx}$) resistivity, and obtain the Hall conductivity as $\sigma_{xy} = \rho_{yx} / (\rho_{xx}^2 + \rho_{yx}^2)$. In Pr$_8$CoGa$_3$, $\sigma_{xy}$ shows a small positive peak at low fields ($B > 0$) and takes negative values at higher magnetic fields. This sign change in $\sigma_{xy}$ indicates parallel conduction of hole-type and electron-type carriers~\cite{Ziman}. We note that the characteristic behavior of $\sigma_{xy}$ persists even above the magnetic transition temperature ($T_N \approx 12 \,\rm{K}$), indicating a minor contribution from magnetism.  



%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%


%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

In the semiclassical two-carrier model, $\sigma_{xy}(B)$ is expressed as
\begin{align}
\sigma_{xy}(B) = \frac{n_{\mathrm{h}} e \mu_\mathrm{h}^2 B}{1 + (\mu_\mathrm{h} B)^2} - n_\mathrm{el} e \mu_\mathrm{el}^2 B,
\end{align}
where $n_\mathrm{h}$ and $\mu_\mathrm{h}$ ($n_\mathrm{el}$ and $\mu_\mathrm{el}$) denote the carrier density and mobility of the hole-type (electron-type) carriers, respectively. We assume the mobility of electron-type carriers is sufficiently low ($\mu_\mathrm{el} B \ll 1$), and estimate $n_\mathrm{el}$ and $\mu_\mathrm{el}$ by also considering the magnitude of $\rho_{xx}$~\cite{SI_preprint}. We perform similar analysis for other \RGa{}, as detailed in Fig.~\ref{Fig_SI_Gxy}~\cite{SI_preprint}.

Figures~\ref{Fig3}(e) and (f) show the extracted hole and electron carrier densities $n_\mathrm{h}$ and $n_\mathrm{el}$. In particular, $n_\mathrm{h}$ of (Ce,Pr,Nd)$_8$CoGa$_3$ is on the order of $10^{16}$-$10^{17}\,$cm$^2/$Vs, which is smaller than $n_\mathrm{el}$ by about four orders of magnitude. %Looking at the \textit{R}-dependence, $n_\mathrm{h}$ monotonically increases with the expansion of $R^{3+}$ radius from Ce to Nd.
The carrier mobility also shows a large difference between hole-type and electron-type carriers: $\mu_\mathrm{h}$ is about $300 \,$cm$^2/$Vs for Ho$_8$CoGa$_3$, but it exceeds $1,000 \,$cm$^2/$Vs for (Ce,Pr,Nd)$_8$CoGa$_3$ even in polycrystalline samples. We attribute the hole-type carriers with high mobility to a small Fermi surface close to the Dirac points. %For the Ce, Pr, and Nd compounds, the two-carrier model is employed due to the observed sign reversal in the Hall conductivity. In contrast, for the Ho and La compounds, a conventional single-carrier model is adopted, as their Hall conductivities exhibit no sign change.
%Figure~\ref{Fig3}(g) displays the carrier mobilities for all compounds. The carriers are categorized into two types: high-mobility Dirac-like carriers and low-mobility trivial carriers. The consistency between the two-carrier analysis at low temperatures and the single-carrier analysis at high temperatures strongly supports the validity of the two-carrier model.

%explain Fig4
We summarize the filling dependence of $\mu_\mathrm{h}$ of \RGa{}, as well as Ho$_8$CoIn$_3$ in Fig.~\ref{Fig4}(a). Here, we convert $n_\mathrm{H}$ to the Fermi energy measured from the Dirac point, $E_\mathrm{F}{}^{\mathrm{Dirac}}$, assuming an isotropic Fermi surface and using the Fermi velocity $v_{\mathrm{F}}{}^{\mathrm{Dirac}}$ from the \textit{ab-initio} calculations~\cite{SI_preprint}. The mobility $\mu_\mathrm{h}$ of various \RX{} shows an increasing trend toward $E_\mathrm{F}{}^{\mathrm{Dirac}} = 0$.
\begin{comment}
Based on the Dirac-like carrier density and the calculated band structure, the Fermi energy measured from the Dirac point, $E_\mathrm{F}{}^{\mathrm{Dirac}}$, is estimated for each compound by the equation
\begin{align}
&n_{\mathrm{Dirac}} = \dfrac{4\pi (k_{\mathrm{F}}{}^{\mathrm{Dirac}})^3}{3} \dfrac{d}{(2\pi)^3}, \\
&E_{\mathrm{F}}{}^{\mathrm{Dirac}} = \hbar v_{\mathrm{F}}{}^{\mathrm{Dirac}} k_{\mathrm{F}}{}^{\mathrm{Dirac}},
\end{align}
where $k_{\mathrm{F}}{}^{\mathrm{Dirac}}$ and $v_{\mathrm{F}}{}^{\mathrm{Dirac}}$ denote the Fermi wave number and Fermi velocity of the Dirac electrons, respectively.
\end{comment}

\textit{Discussion} -- We first examine the dependence of $E_\mathrm{F}{}^{\mathrm{Dirac}}$ on the ionic radius $r_{R^{3+}}$. Except for Ho$_8$CoGa$_3$, the estimated $E_\mathrm{F}{}^{\mathrm{Dirac}}$ monotonically decreases with increasing ionic radius in the order of Ho$_8$CoIn$_3$ and (Nd/Pr/Ce)$_8$CoGa$_3$, as shown in Fig.~\ref{Fig4}(a), Ho$_8$CoGa$_3$ is likely to have a different magnitude of deficiency. The systematic $r_{R^{3+}}$ dependence of $E_\mathrm{F}{}^{\mathrm{Dirac}}$ indicates the successful tuning of the Fermi energy to the Dirac crossing point via bandwidth control in \RX{} (see the insets to Fig.~\ref{Fig4}(a)).

Next, we elaborate on the relationship between $\mu_\mathrm{h}$ and $E_\mathrm{F}{}^{\mathrm{Dirac}}$.  In our experiments, these quantities are found to be inversely proportional to each other, which is well understood in the context of the linear energy dispersion near the Dirac crossing points: The mobility of charge carriers with a linear band dispersion is given by
\begin{equation}
\mu = \frac{e \tau}{m^*}=\frac{e \tau  (v_{\mathrm{F}}{}^{\mathrm{Dirac}})^2}{ E_{\mathrm{F}}{}^{\mathrm{Dirac}}},
\label{eq:mu_linear}
\end{equation}
since the effective mass is expressed as $m^* = \hbar k_{\mathrm{F}}{}^{\mathrm{Dirac}} / {v_{\mathrm{F}}{}^{\mathrm{Dirac}}}$, considering momentum conservation. Here, $\hbar$, $k_{\mathrm{F}}$ and $\tau$ are Planck's constant divided by $2\pi$, the Fermi wave number, and the carrier relaxation time, respectively. The inverse proportionality between $\mu_\mathrm{h}$ and $E_\mathrm{F}{}^{\mathrm{Dirac}}$ is consistently explained by Eq.~(\ref{eq:mu_linear}), supporting the existence of Dirac fermions with a linear band dispersion in \RX{}.


 \vspace{3mm}
 %Summary
\textit{Conclusions} -- {In summary, we demonstrate bandwidth control by \textit{R}-site substitution for the covalent-type, three-dimensional Dirac semimetals \RX{}. We observe parallel conduction of Dirac-like and trivial electrons by electronic transport measurements, and successfully extract the transport properties of the Dirac electrons by fits to a two-carrier model. The Fermi energy is successfully tuned by increasing the ionic radius $r_{\mathrm{R}^{3+}}$ of \textit{R} atoms; the carrier mobility is enhanced as the inverse of the Fermi energy measured from the Dirac point, consistent with the linear-in-energy character of the band dispersion. In Fig.~\ref{Fig4}(b), we compare the observed physical parameters of our \RX{} compounds, which are covalent-type, three-dimensional DSMs, with representative spin-orbit type DSMs~\cite{Na3Bi_band_calculation, Cd3As2_band, ZrTe5_transport, ZrTe5_band}. Our experiments establish the existence of a new class of DSMs in a regime of weak spin–orbit coupling that has so far received little attention, opening up a promising direction beyond the well-studied spin–orbit type topological metals~\cite{Topological_semimetals_prediction_2016,topological_materials_discovery_2019}. %despite prior, large-scale searches for spin-orbit type topological metals~\cite{Topological_semimetals_prediction_2016,topological_materials_discovery_2019}. 
Combined with the covalent-type nodal-line topological semimetals in square-net with more dense 4$^4$ net materials which possess half-filled \textit{p}$_x$ and \textit{p}$_y$ bands~\cite{topological_in_square_net, delocalized_role_in_square_net, square_net_topology_2022, SrMnBi2_Dirac_fermions, ZrSiS_Dirac_fermions}, covalent-type topological semimetals can be ideal platforms for systematic investigation of topological phenomena with wide chemical and electronic tunability.}  
