The following example shows that the finitary constraint in \Cref{th:cox.lip} matters.

\begin{example}\label{ex:inf.dihedr}
  Let $(W,S)=(I_2(\infty),\ \{a,b\})$ be the infinite dihedral group realized as a Coxeter system in the usual fashion \cite[Example 1.2.7]{bb_comb-cox}, with $a$ and $b$ involutions satisfying no other relations. The non-trivial elements of $W$ are words on the alphabet $\{a,b\}$ with alternating letters, and the map
  \begin{equation*}
    \tau_w
    =
    \begin{cases}
      1&\text{if $w=1$}\\
      1&\text{if $w=\cdots ba$}\\
      w&\text{if $w=\cdots ab$}
    \end{cases}
  \end{equation*}
  is easily seen to be $T$-Lipschitz; it is of course neither a right translation nor constant.
\end{example}

