\begin{abstract}
% Recent studies have demonstrated the strong potential of large language models (LLMs) in hardware generation. However, current approaches for generating RTL code from natural language still face several challenges: (1) Supervised fine-tuning can not ensure the model-generated Verilog code is optimal, lacking consideration of power, performance, and area (PPA). (2) Post-processing methods to search the PPA-optimal RTL including retrieval augmented generation, Monte Carlo Tree Search and FunSearch, will lead to increased inference time under identical workloads. (3) Current works lack comprehensive optimization simultaneously for Verilog syntax, functional correctness and PPA performance.


% To address these limitations, we propose a hierarchical reward-driven reinforcement learning framework \textbf{ChipSeek-R1}. By leveraging our framework, the model is encouraged to generate RTL code that is not only functionally correct but also optimized for performance with reasoning capability. Experimental results show that our framework achieves SOTA effects in functional correctness and discovers 27 out of 44 RTL designs with better PPA performance than the original human-written code on the RTLLM benchmark.

Large Language Models (LLMs) show significant potential for automating Register-Transfer Level (RTL) code generation. However, current approaches face a critical challenge: they can not simultaneously optimize for functional correctness and hardware quality (Power, Performance, Area - PPA). Methods based on supervised fine-tuning often generate functionally correct but PPA-suboptimal code, lacking mechanisms to learn optimization principles. In contrast, post-processing techniques that attempt to improve PPA metrics after generation are often inefficient because they operate externally without updating the LLM's parameters, thus failing to enhance the model's intrinsic design capabilities.
% This difficulty stems fundamentally from a lack of integrated feedback from simulation and synthesis tools during the model's training process. 

To bridge this gap, we introduce ChipSeek-R1, a hierarchical reward-driven reinforcement learning framework to train LLMs to generate RTL code that achieves both functional correctness and optimized PPA metrics. ChipSeek-R1 employs a hierarchical reward system, which incorporates direct feedback on syntax, functional correctness (from simulators) and PPA metrics (from synthesis tools) during reinforcement learning. This enables the model to learn complex hardware design trade-offs via trial-and-error, generating RTL code that is both functionally correct and PPA-optimized. Evaluating ChipSeek-R1 on standard benchmarks (VerilogEval, RTLLM), we achieve state-of-the-art results in functional correctness. Notably, on the RTLLM benchmark, ChipSeek-R1 generated 27 RTL designs surpassing the PPA metrics of the original human-written code. Our findings demonstrate the effectiveness of integrating toolchain feedback into LLM training and highlight the potential for reinforcement learning to enable automated generation of human-surpassing RTL code. We open-source our code in \href{https://anonymous.4open.science/r/ChipSeek-R1}{anonymous.4open.science}.
\end{abstract}

