\section{Toy Example: Finger Counting}
\label{Sec:ToyFingerCounting}

This example illustrates how replay and hierarchical memory enable the model to bind structured inputs (e.g. finger gestures) to symbolic tokens (e.g. number words).

\subsection{Modalities and Embeddings}

We assume two input modalities:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Letter modality:} Basis tokens \( v_k \) for alphabet letters (e.g., \( v_{\bf o}, v_{\bf n}, v_{\bf e} \)).
    \item \textbf{Finger modality:} Scalars \( u \in \{0,1\} \) indicating raised fingers.
\end{itemize}

Each embedding \( a_\alpha \) is associated with hierarchical memory:
\[
m_{\alpha} = \sum_{n} \sum_{\mu_n} m^{(n)}_{\alpha,\mu_n} \tilde{v}^{(n)}_{\mu_n}
\]
For example, the word ``{\bf one}'' is stored as:
\begin{eqnarray}
m_1 &=& m_{1,\bf o}^{(1)}v_{\bf o} + m_{1,\bf n}^{(1)}v_{\bf n} + m_{1,\bf e}^{(1)}v_{\bf e} \notag\\
&+& m_{1,\bf on}^{(2)}\tilde{v}_{\bf on}^{(2)} + m_{1,\bf ne}^{(2)}\tilde{v}_{\bf ne}^{(2)} + m_{1,\bf one}^{(3)}\tilde{v}_{\bf one}^{(3)}, \notag
\end{eqnarray}
and similarly for ``two'' and ``three''.

Finger gestures are retokenized as:
\[
\tilde{u}^{(n)} = \prod_{x=1}^{n} u_x,
\]
with $\tilde{u}^{(n)}=1$ if $n$ fingers are raised.

\subsection{Binding Fingers to Numbers}

We define the Hamiltonian:
\begin{align}
H &= \sum_{\alpha} a_\alpha \left( \sum_n \sum_{\mu_n} m^{(n)}_{\alpha, \mu_n} \, \tilde{v}^{(n)}_{\mu_n}\right) \label{Eqn:FingerCounting:KV}\\
&\quad + G_{1}^{(1)} \, a_1 \, u + G_{2}^{(2)} \, a_2 \, \tilde{u}^{(2)} + G_{3}^{(3)} \, a_3 \, \tilde{u}^{(3)} . 
\end{align}
where the $G$ couplings are learned via Hebbian updates, e.g. showing {\bf two} and two raised fingers. (Technically, replay will bind $\alpha>n$ embeddings to $\tilde{u}^{(n)}$, such as $a_3$ being bound to $u$, $\tilde{u}^{(2)}$, and $\tilde{u}^{(3)}$. But this interference is mitigated by biasing $G_{\alpha}^{(n)}\rightarrow \beta^nG_{\alpha}^{(n)}$ with $\beta>1$, and choosing $f_{\alpha}$ to be normalization.)

During inference we raise 2 fingers ($\tilde{u}^{(n)}=\sum_{n'\leq 2}\delta_{n',n}$), 
\[
\Delta a_\alpha = G_\alpha^{(n)} \tilde{u}^{(n)} \quad \Rightarrow \quad a_\alpha \mapsto \text{``two''} \Rightarrow v_k = t,w,o.
\]
That is, a finger count activates the corresponding embedding, which then drives autoregressive symbol generation following the key-value relationship encoded in Eqn \ref{Eqn:FingerCounting:KV}.




