    % As Artificial Intelligence (AI) transitions towards collaborative roles in creative endeavors, the concept of agency becomes paramount in defining and understanding these new interaction dynamics. Characterizing how agency manifests and is distributed between human and AI partners is crucial for effectively navigating this shift from AI-as-tool to AI-as-collaborator. However, despite the centrality of agency, research currently lacks a comprehensive, systematic examination of its role and configurations within human-AI co-creation. Addressing this critical gap, this paper conducts a systematic review of 134 works from top-tier HCI conferences (CHI, UIST, CSCW) in the past 20 years, specifically investigating the manifestation and distribution of agency within these collaborative systems. Through this review, we structure the landscape of human-AI co-creation: we first synthesize the prevalent forms of agency (passive to cooperative) observed in these systems, then systematically categorize the control mechanisms governing agency allocation, and finally, we construct a three-layer framework to elucidate the intricate connections between specific agency configurations, control strategies, and their observed impacts across diverse application domains. This integrated analysis offered concrete guidance for future research and system design in human-AI co-creation through the lens of agency.
