%\subsection{The configuration value for airport games: numerical results}



We can draw some conclusions from the last column of Table \ref{dataSCQ}. Aircraft movements operated by airlines that use an airport occasionally face higher movement fees, while those operating frequently benefit from lower fees. For example, A320 aircraft operated solely by $A_{15}$ and $A_{18}$ incur significantly higher fees compared to those operated by $A_2$ and $A_3$, which have many movements. Smaller aircraft like the LJ45 also exhibit a fee per operation similar to that for B38M and higher than that for B738, both operated by  $A_1$ and corresponding to the two largest aircraft types. Since fees are charged per movement, airlines with frequent operations can distribute costs more effectively, leading to lower per-movement fees. However, cooperation among airlines affects cost allocation. For instance, $A_3$ (Iberia) collaborates widely, resulting in lower fees for A320 and A20N movements, whereas the same aircraft types operated by $A_4$, which does not operate under code-sharing agreements, face much higher fees.



