Numerical solvers for PDEs remain indispensable for modeling spatiotemporal systems but are often prohibitively expensive for real-time applications. While ML surrogates offer faster alternatives, their autoregressive implementations suffer from compounding errors and sensitivity to hyperparameters, issues that limit their reliability in long-term forecasting. By revisiting a core tenet of ensemble learning - \textit{diversity through randomization} - we address these limitations. {\color{red} But we shouldn't really claim that we address the hyperparameter problem here, should we?} Our framework trains multiple machine learning (ML) surrogates in parallel, utilizing random initializations to bypass the traditional trial-and-error process of selecting a single "best" model. {\color{red}if we are looking at random initializations, is this really something that people use trial-and-error to find the best model? I think of this more as in hyperparameter tuning, which we aren't addressing here.} This approach not only reduces error accumulation by $15$ to $33\%$ across three distinct PDE-driven systems (stress fields, reaction-diffusion, and planetary fluid dynamics) but also aligns with the growing need for computationally efficient, ``plug-and-play" ML tools in scientific domains.\\
\indent The success of our method stems from its inherent robustness to the non-convexity of neural network loss landscapes. By leveraging the stochasticity of training dynamics, the deep ensemble ensemble captures diverse approximations of the underlying PDE solution space, mitigating biases inherent to individual models. Crucially, the framework retains the computational advantages of ML surrogates, achieving inference significantly faster than numerical solvers while requiring only a few initial time-steps data - a critical feature for real-time applications like climate modeling or material design.\\
\indent Future work could extend this paradigm by integrating physics-informed architectures to regularize ensemble predictions further or by optimizing initialization strategies to maximize model diversity. {\color{red}Or could we actually propose to use deep ensemble with varying hyperparameters?}

