Taking into account that $\mathrm{Fin}$ is a $F_\sigma$-ideal (since it is a countable family), it follows that the set $\mathrm{HC}(T)$ of ordinary hypercyclic vectors  
%(that is,  $\mathrm{HC}_T(\mathrm{Fin})$) 
is either empty or a dense $G_\delta$-set, hence either empty or comeager. A similar claim can be shown for the ideal $\mathsf{Z}$ of asymptotic density zero sets (which is an analytic $P$-ideal, but not $F_\sigma$): the set $\mathrm{UFHC}(T)$ of upper frequently hypercyclic vectors 
%(that is, $\mathrm{HC}_T(\mathsf{Z})$) 
is either empty or contains a dense $G_{\delta}$-set, hence either empty or comeager, see e.g. \cite[Theorem 3.7]{Leo24}. 

%If, in addition, $X$ is Baire, then $\mathrm{UFHC}(T)$ is either empty or contains a dense $G_{\delta}$-set (hence, comeager). 

