To this aim, consider the Baire space $\omega^\omega$ (which is a zero-dimensional Polish space, once it is endowed with the product topology of the discrete topology on $\omega$) and define %the subset
$$
C_3:=\{x \in \omega^\omega: \lim_{n\to \infty} x_n=+\infty\}.
$$
It is well known that $C_3$ is $F_{\sigma\delta}$ but not $G_{\delta\sigma}$, see \cite[Definition 22.9 and Exercise 23.2]{MR1321597}. Next, define also the closed set 
$$
\Delta:=\{x \in \omega^\omega: x_n\le n \text{ for all }n \in \omega\},
$$
and observe that $\Delta$ is a Polish space on its own by Alexandrov's theorem, see e.g. \cite[Proposition 3.7 and Theorem 3.11]{MR1321597}.
\begin{claim}\label{claim:complexityD}
    $D:=\Delta\setminus C_3$ is $G_{\delta\sigma}$ but not $F_{\sigma\delta}$ in $\Delta$. 
\end{claim}
\begin{proof}
    Notice that $\Delta\setminus D=\Delta \cap C_3$ is the intersection of two $F_{\sigma\delta}$ sets in $\omega^\omega$, hence also in $\Delta$. Hence $D$ is $G_{\delta\sigma}$ in $\Delta$. Moreover, the map $h: \omega^\omega\to \Delta$ defined by $h(x):=(\min\{n,x_n\}: n \in \omega)$ is continuous and $h^{-1}[\Delta \cap C_3]=C_3$. This implies that $\Delta \cap C_3$ is not $G_{\delta\sigma}$ in $\Delta$, hence its complement $D$ is not $F_{\sigma\delta}$ in $\Delta$. 
\end{proof}    

Our proof strategy will be the construction of a continuous function 
$$
